KZ3 patch 2092: device-resident vertex cache (LD_VTX_DEV=1|0, LD_VTX_DEV_MB, LD_VTX_DEV_STAGE_MB); pass 87.

Built on patch 2047's draw payload cache (which LD_VTX_DEV=1 turns on, main memory included): a cached draw's
converted vertices and topology indices live in one DEFAULT-heap arena buffer instead of a CPU copy, so a hit costs
no fetch, no ring copy and no PCIe read of the UPLOAD heap. The fill draw draws from the upload ring as before; its
payload and indices are also written to a staging block of an own UPLOAD buffer and a GPU copy into the arena is
queued; the copy list (patch 2053's, created for this switch too) runs it ahead of the submit's draw list. A hit
binds the arena block as vertex and index buffer. Validity is 2047's rule, unchanged in meaning: no tracked write
(cellGcm_note_guest_write_pages_ea, SPU puts, host file reads, the walker's own stores: the 64 KB page bitmap and
stamps) since the fill, then a full compare of every source byte against the copy taken at the fill (recompiled PPU
stores into main memory note nothing). A dropped entry's arena block and every staging block return to their
allocator only when the fence of the lists that may still read them completes. Also for 2047: two ways per set
instead of direct mapping (the less recently looked-up way is evicted), and with LD_VTX_DEV an entry invalidated
twice by content changes is dynamic like one invalidated twice by tracked writes. Off with the dumps that read every
draw's CPU payload, LD_GEOM_CAPTURE and LD_VTX_CACHE=verify. Log `[vtx-dev]` (cumulative, with `[vtx-cache]`).

Host test: tools/smoke/test_vtx_dev.sh (arena with fence-deferred frees under a 200k-step random model, the
tracked-write and full-compare hit rule; no D3D12).

